Research Proposal
Title
Selective Claim-Level Auditing for Detecting Unsupported Claims in Retrieval-Augmented Generation
Alternative project name:
RAGGuard-Lite: A Runtime Claim-Level Auditor for Evidence-Grounded RAG Answers
________________________________________
1. Problem Statement
Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems are widely used to answer questions over documents, policies, PDFs, manuals, websites, and enterprise knowledge bases. In theory, RAG should reduce hallucination because the model receives retrieved evidence before generating an answer. In practice, RAG systems still produce answers that contain unsupported claims, partially supported claims, contradictions, outdated information, or claims that go beyond the retrieved context.
Most RAG evaluation systems produce answer-level or pipeline-level scores such as faithfulness, relevance, context precision, or answer correctness. These scores are useful for benchmarking, but they are not always actionable for a real user or developer. If a RAG answer is risky, the user needs to know which exact claim is unsupported and which evidence span was used to judge it.
This project proposes a claim-level auditing system that takes a user question, retrieved context chunks, and a generated RAG answer, then identifies atomic claims, retrieves the most relevant evidence span for each claim, verifies whether each claim is supported, and produces an answer-level hallucination risk score.
________________________________________
2. Core Research Gap
Existing RAG evaluation systems can detect or estimate hallucination, but many of them either:
1.	evaluate the answer as a whole,
2.	rely on scalar faithfulness scores,
3.	require reference answers,
4.	do not localize unsupported claims clearly,
5.	do not separate decomposition quality from verification quality,
6.	are expensive if every sentence is decomposed and judged by a large LLM,
7.	do not provide a simple runtime audit report for each claim.
The proposed research gap is:
Existing RAG evaluation approaches do not sufficiently study whether selectively decomposing only complex answer sentences into atomic claims can improve unsupported-claim detection while reducing verification cost compared to full decomposition.
This gives the project a narrow and measurable contribution.
________________________________________
3. Main Research Question
Does selective claim decomposition improve claim-level hallucination detection in RAG answers compared to sentence-level verification and full atomic decomposition?
________________________________________
4. Secondary Research Questions
RQ1
Does selective claim decomposition improve unsupported-claim detection compared to verifying complete sentences directly?
RQ2
Does selective decomposition reduce cost compared to decomposing every sentence into atomic claims?
RQ3
Does claim-level evidence-span retrieval improve support classification compared to verifying claims against the full retrieved context?
RQ4
Can claim-level support labels be aggregated into an answer-level risk score that predicts whether a RAG answer is hallucinated?
________________________________________
5. Proposed Contribution
The project contributes a lightweight auditing pipeline with four main components:
1.	Selective Claim Decomposition
o	Only complex sentences are decomposed into smaller atomic claims.
o	Simple factual sentences are preserved as single claims.
o	This reduces unnecessary claim splitting, lowers LLM cost, and avoids noisy over-decomposition.
2.	Evidence-Span Retrieval
o	Retrieved RAG chunks are further split into smaller evidence units.
o	Each claim is matched with its most relevant evidence spans using hybrid sparse and dense retrieval.
3.	Grounded Claim Verification
o	Each claim-evidence pair is classified as supported, partially supported, unsupported, or contradicted.
o	A compact verifier is used first.
o	A stronger LLM judge is only used for uncertain cases.
4.	Answer-Level Risk Aggregation
o	Claim-level labels are aggregated into a final answer risk score.
o	The output is explainable because each risk score is traceable to specific unsupported claims.
________________________________________
6. What the System Does Not Do
To keep the project realistic and defensible, the first version will not build:
•	a new RAG chatbot,
•	a new retriever,
•	a new large language model,
•	a full answer rewriting system,
•	a correction engine,
•	a browser-based search system,
•	a large human-labeled dataset from scratch.
The system audits already-generated RAG answers.
