9. Datasets
Primary Dataset 1: RAGTruth
Use for:
•	hallucination detection,
•	answer-level labels,
•	span-level hallucination mapping,
•	evaluating whether claim-level labels align with hallucinated spans.
Why useful:
•	It is directly about RAG hallucination.
•	It contains real generated RAG responses.
•	It includes manually annotated hallucination information.
How to use it:
1.	Load question, retrieved context, and generated answer.
2.	Use word-level hallucination annotations to infer whether extracted claims overlap hallucinated spans.
3.	Mark a claim unsupported if a significant portion overlaps a hallucinated span.
4.	Use answer-level labels for answer risk evaluation.
Possible limitation:
•	It may not provide clean atomic claim labels directly.
•	You may need to convert word-level annotations into claim-level labels.
________________________________________
Primary Dataset 2: AttributionBench
Use for:
•	claim-level attribution classification,
•	supported vs unsupported claim verification,
•	evaluating whether evidence supports a claim.
Why useful:
•	It is directly aligned with your claim-verification task.
•	It contains query, claim, evidence, and attribution labels.
•	It is suitable for testing support classification.
How to use it:
1.	Use its claim-evidence pairs directly.
2.	Train or evaluate claim verification models.
3.	Use in-distribution and out-of-distribution splits if available.
4.	Report macro-F1, supported F1, and unsupported F1.
Possible limitation:
•	It is more attribution-focused than full RAG auditing.
•	It may not represent all RAG answer styles.
________________________________________
Primary Dataset 3: LLM-AggreFact / MiniCheck Benchmark
Use for:
•	grounded claim verification,
•	comparing against compact fact-checking models,
•	testing generalization beyond one dataset.
Why useful:
•	It is built for checking whether generated claims are grounded in evidence.
•	It gives a strong baseline direction.
•	It supports the cost-efficiency angle.
How to use it:
1.	Use claim-document pairs.
2.	Evaluate the verifier module separately.
3.	Compare against MiniCheck or NLI-based baselines.
________________________________________
Optional Dataset 4: GaRAGe
Use only if time allows.
Use for:
•	grounding passage selection,
•	long-form answer grounding,
•	deflection when evidence is insufficient.
Why useful:
•	It is closer to real-world RAG.
•	It contains grounding annotations.
•	It tests whether models can identify relevant grounding passages.
Why not use first:
•	It adds complexity.
•	It may slow down the MVP.
•	It is better for extension after the core system works.
