19. Passing Gates
Gate 1: Scope Gate
Pass if:
The project can be explained as:
"Given a RAG answer and retrieved evidence, the system identifies unsupported claims and produces a risk score."
Fail if:
The project becomes a full RAG chatbot, search engine, or answer correction system.
________________________________________
Gate 2: Dataset Gate
Pass if:
At least 1,000 normalized claim-level examples are available.
At least 1,000 answer-level examples are available.
All examples follow one JSON schema.
Fail if:
Datasets cannot be loaded or labels cannot be mapped consistently.
________________________________________
Gate 3: Baseline Gate
Pass if:
At least four baselines run end-to-end.
Metrics are generated automatically.
Results are saved in CSV/JSON format.
Minimum baselines:
sentence-level verifier
full decomposition
RAGAS faithfulness
MiniCheck or NLI verifier
________________________________________
Gate 4: Decomposition Gate
Pass if manual inspection shows:
At least 80% of extracted claims are understandable.
At least 75% are atomic enough to verify.
Less than 20% are duplicates or useless.
Fail if:
Claim extraction creates too many broken or meaningless claims.
________________________________________
Gate 5: Evidence Retrieval Gate
Pass if:
Top-3 evidence contains useful support for at least 75% of supported claims in a manually checked subset.
Fail if:
Evidence retrieval usually misses the relevant span.
________________________________________
Gate 6: Main Result Gate
Pass if at least one is true:
Selective decomposition improves unsupported F1 over sentence-level verification.
Selective decomposition matches full decomposition with lower cost.
Selective decomposition improves answer-level AUROC over unsupported-ratio baseline.
Fail if:
Selective decomposition is worse than all baselines and does not reduce cost.
________________________________________
Gate 7: Paper Gate
Pass if the final paper contains:
clear research question
related work comparison
method diagram
dataset table
main results table
ablation table
cost table
error analysis
limitations
Fail if:
The paper only describes a tool without controlled experiments.
